WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.440
A finished neuron hides its past.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.520
Which label could reveal the part you need in that tissue?

3
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.520
Its shape alone cannot show its birth time, ancestry, or distant projection.

4
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.640
Each question needs an event that can carry that information forward.

5
00:00:23.217 --> 00:00:24.517
Start with identity.

6
00:00:25.937 --> 00:00:28.257
A Nissl stain reveals many cell bodies.

7
00:00:29.457 --> 00:00:32.337
A Golgi stain fills a few cells and their branches.

8
00:00:33.777 --> 00:00:35.977
An antibody binds one chosen protein.

9
00:00:37.377 --> 00:00:40.697
These marks report what they detect, not when the cells formed.

10
00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:44.847
For a projection, use the neuron's own transport.

11
00:00:46.187 --> 00:00:50.487
Put a tracer near cell bodies, and anterograde transport carries it toward

12
00:00:50.487 --> 00:00:55.967
terminals. Put a retrograde tracer at a target, and terminals carry it back.

13
00:00:57.227 --> 00:01:00.767
The result maps a sampled population, not every synapse.

14
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.020
Birth-dating follows a different event.

15
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:11.520
During a short pulse, tritiated thymidine or BrdU enters DNA being synthesized.

16
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.820
In a dividing cell, that means S phase.

17
00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.660
The pulse marks a cohort copying DNA during that window.

18
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.450
After the pulse comes the chase.

19
00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:27.490
Each further division usually dilutes label among the descendants.

20
00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:34.010
Heavy label later supports few additional divisions and an approximate last S phase.

21
00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:40.150
Weak label fits continued cycling, although uptake and detection also vary.

22
00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:44.323
Ancestry needs a mark that survives division.

23
00:01:45.663 --> 00:01:49.283
A grafted quail nucleus stays recognizable in chick tissue.

24
00:01:50.563 --> 00:01:53.903
A retroviral insert enters a dividing cell's DNA.

25
00:01:55.703 --> 00:01:57.663
Cre can permanently switch on a reporter.

26
00:01:59.003 --> 00:02:02.023
In each case, descendants inherit a durable difference.

27
00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:04.583
Predict the next frame.

28
00:02:05.843 --> 00:02:09.643
One founder carries a permanent DNA change, then divides twice.

29
00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:12.483
Which cells should carry that change?

30
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.220
Every descendant inherits the changed DNA.

31
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.640
Yet one colour across many founders gives a population fate map, not separate

32
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.900
clones. Sparse induction, multiple colours, or distinct barcodes can separate

33
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:35.900
neighbouring families.

34
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.860
Here is the rule. A tool supports only what its mark records.

35
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.820
Protein binding can report target detection.

36
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.860
Transport can report a projection.

37
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.140
DNA synthesis can date a cohort approximately.

38
00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.660
An inherited DNA change can report ancestry.

39
00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.100
The rule also tells you when to stop.

40
00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.540
A tracer does not prove every synapse.

41
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:08.380
A weak birth label is not a precise birthday.

42
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.220
A shared lineage colour may hide many founders.

43
00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.980
State the strongest claim, then name the limit.
